Date: Fri, 15 Oct 93 11:11:34 -0400
From: Tim Freeman <tsf@cs.cmu.edu>
Subject: BURGLARY: Razor wire?

In a recent post to the political section of the cryonics
mailing list (CRYOMSG 0014.342), there was a brief description of a
building that Alcor decided not to move to.  One of the reasons for
not moving there (among many) was that neighboring buldings had "razor
wire" on the exterior; presumably this is for the purpose of slicing
up people foolish enough to attempt to climb the building and to try
to enter through the roof.  I suppose this is relevant because it
means there is a lot of crime in that area.

Does anyone know of companies that sell this stuff and of clever ways
to use it for domestic defense?

I was under the impression that a property owner can be sued if a
criminal is stupid enough to injure himself while burglarizing the
property.  Is this so?  For instance, I've been told that the trick of
installing razor blades on the back of your car radio to greet the
person trying to steal it is a bad idea because of the harm it would
do to the thief and the legal repercussions of this, not only because
of the harm it might do to kids playing in the car or the guy you
hired to repair your radio.

One difference might be that the razor blades on the car radio are
hidden, and the razor wire outside these buildings is presumably
well-labeled, since it was clearly visible in a video tape.  Does the
sneakyness of the booby traps you set for the criminal make a
legal difference, or is this just a practical safety issue?

(I rent my apartment and my car radio is practically worthless, so I'm
not considering doing anything clever now.  But it would be good to
know what is practical.)

Tim

