Date: Fri, 15 Oct 93 16:27:12 GMT
From: "Stephen J. Whitrow" <sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk>
Subject: QUANTUM: Many Minds Interpretation (was: free will)

Paul Cisek says:

>I have a difficult time believing a theory which requires such postulates as
>the existence of a vast number of superimposed parallel universes to explain
>something like the paradoxes of quantum physics.  (A question, has the "EPR
>Paradox", which you referred to as a thought experiment, been actually
>tested?  Does it not require completely non-intrusive measurement methods?).

In the eighties, the experiments of Alain Aspect in Paris were aimed at
testing whether quantum theory or an alternative called "hidden variable"
would make the most accurate predictions.  The EPR paradoxes were about
non-local correlations.  For instance, after two spinning particles
interact, each one has an equal but opposite value of "spin"; if one is
"up" then the other is "down".  According to quantum theory, though, both
particles are both "up and down", and do not become "up" or "down" until
a measurement is made.  Then if you measured one particle, the other one
would suddenly "know" which state it should take up, even if they were
outside of each other's light cone.

Polarisation is to photons (or a beam of photons) as spin is to material
particles -- it's the equivalent property.  There are many atomic
processes which will produce two photons travelling in opposite
directions, and when this happens the polarizations of the two photons
is always correlated.  The latest experiments by Aspect involved making
the "decision" as to which way to measure the polarizations only after
the photons were flying.  A light beam was passed through a switch 
which could direct the beam to either of two polarizing filters, each
measuring a different type of polarization.  Every 10nS the direction
of the beam through the switch was controlled by a pseudorandom signal.
There was no way in which any signal concerning the setup could travel 
between parts of the apparatus to affect the measurement, since it took
20nS for photons to travel from the atom where they were created to the
detectors.  The experiments were found to be in accordance with the
predictions by quantum theory.

>You seem to suggest that evolution has selected those species that had
>extension in "reality space".  As an example, you give the selective advantage
>of color discernment which you claim is better in an extended mind than in one
>that is limited to a single deterministic universe.  However, your argument
>about the properties of light in a single universe depends upon the many worlds
>interpretation, and thus gives no support for that interpretation (except to
>show that it may be self-consistent).  Don't get me wrong, it's an interesting
>notion, I just don't find it necessary.  (Are you saying that if there's one
>universe then it must be deterministic?  If so, why?)

Quantum mechanics has been fully validated as a scientific theory,
following experiments where its predictions were borne out (see above).
So the question then boils down to which philosophical interpretation
you prefer.  The standard interpretation claims that "conscious observers"
can "collapse wavefunctions" by some sort of psychokinesis -- their minds
instantaneously force faraway photons to take on a particular
polarisation, or force faraway leptons to take on either a fuzzily
defined position and clearly defined momentum or the other way round,
when previously both momenta and position were fuzzy.  And the other
interesting fact is that there is said to be a point where an observer
suddenly becomes "conscious enough to collapse wavefunctions".  A cat,
for instance, does not qualify as a "conscious observer" as it is claimed 
to be not "clever enough" to know whether it is alive or dead!  John von
Neumann postulated that the wavefunction collapses on observation, but
no mechanism for its collapse was proposed.  No one has ever been able
to explain how faraway matter can communicate, any more than they can 
explain how a voodooist can make someone ill by sticking pins in an
effigy.

So the standard interpretation is not scientific -- it violates the laws
of causality and relativity, and is unable to explain how and why a
particle chooses which way to go from the quantum probabilities.  Events
like radioactive decay are said not to happen for a reason, but according
to "probabilistic" laws.  It has to assume the existence of weird things
called "particle-waves".  And the universe's wavefunction can never be
"collapsed", unless an external observer is postulated.  Cosmologists
are unhappy with this as you need "God" as the external observer.  There
are other alternatives which are just as mystical.  Usually there is the
notion that everything in the universe is holistically interconnected.
You could have a panpsychic universe, where particles have mental states
and are also in telepathic contact with other particles, able to
instantly correlate their behaviour like a flock of birds.  Or maybe the
universe is full of Leibniz's "windowless monads" which "mirror the
universe".  Alternatively the pantheists could be right and the whole
universe is "God".  The  Brahman / Dharmakaya / Tao might be behind it
all, or perhaps we're just fleas on a turtles's back....

A scientific theory has to be self-consistent and in accordance with
observed phenomena, so the above interpretations may be rejected as
nonsense.  Unless we go the way of Bishop Berkeley, we already have
to assume the existence of at least one material world.  With no
material world we can only explain things in terms of a "spirit"
world.  But with a single material world we still have to bestow
minds and "mental powers" with the ability to effect "ghostly action 
at a distance", given the results of quantum experiments.  It's hardly
better than Berkeley's view.  A theory which allows for a large number
of material worlds is a more materialistic theory, since we are better
able to explain things in physical terms.

Since all possible permutations of reality can exist (by definition),
to conclude that one of them is the One True Reality appears to be
presupposing that some superbeing stands in judgement outside of all
these universes, and makes a pronouncement on which of them is real,
and disqualifies the rest as "simulations".  We could imagine the
inhabitants of other universes equally sure that their version is the
correct one, where World War Two never broke out for instance, and
finding the idea of "other realities" rather amusing.  But as their
scientific knowledge advanced, they would start to realise that such
an egocentric Ptolemic view is mistaken.

I would have an "omniverse", containing all possible permutations of
reality.  Since supernatural beings, by definition, violate natural law,
universes which contain them are invalid and hence non-existent by
anybody's standards.  Any given individual exists in a small subset of
all possible worlds, call it their multiverse.  A multiverse contains
all those universes where the individual's brain is identical enough
physically not to allow a divergence of consciousness.  So normally the
universes would all be fairly similar, only distinguished by those things
that are unknown to the individual, and the brain would be superposed in
near enough the same location in physical space.  Some universes, though,
could have him as a brain in a vat in the Andromeda Galaxy, fooled
into thinking he was on a fictitious Earth, say, and the genuine and
simulated versions would be unable to tell themselves apart!

Each universe is totally deterministic.  In one universe a particle goes
one way at a particular branch, in another it takes another path. The
whole lot of universes in a multiverse take on a distribution in
accordance with the quantum probability laws and forms a wavefunction;
so these laws just arise from the sum of permutations of reality that
are occupied by an observer's brain.  Matter appears to have a wave and
particle nature; the particle nature only appears when you can nail down
the particle's exact position.  But all that's happening is a
differentiation of the observer's consciousness stream as his brains go
their separate ways in "reality space".  There's really no such thing as
a "particle-wave", reality is more in line with people's common sense.  

As for whether a single universe could be non-deterministic, I'd say it
was unlikely.  You'd end up with the same problems as Copenhagenism, 
having to postulate mystical points of view.  It would be like a two-
dimensional Flatland creature trying to explain how a car worked, 
without resorting to mysticism.  If it could just expand its perspective,
it might be able to come up with a materialistic theory.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

